>First, at the end of every game (well, at least games where I don't die 
>early), I have piles of unidentified wands, potions and scrolls.  I'm 
>scared to use any of them randomly because I don't know what they do.

That is good, in general. In dire situations, trying out random stuff is
better than dying without trying.
 
>I've been told (by people in #nethack, on freenode) that the only way to 
>identify them is by a scroll of identify.  

There are several notions of "identify". There is (1) "identify" in the
sense that the game will tell you that runed wands /are/ wands of
striking, and there is (2) "identify" in the sense that /you/ know that
runed wands are wands of strinking. To support (2) in the absense of (1),
there is the #name command, with which you can name (call) classes of
items.

There are many ways to identify items in the sense of (2). For example,
because the amount of gold payed by shopkeepers for items is constant
across games, you can group items into several price categories. (To use
this effectively, you would need to look at spoilerish price tables).
Also, wands can be identified relatively easily: Ratenir jvgu gurz ba na
rkvfgvat ratenirzrag. There is also a way to identify rings, but you
might lose them. Consider that monsters throwing potions at you will not
throw, say, potions of full healing.

